    "label": "Psychological, Social, and Biological Foundations of Behavior",
    "examId": "ps90_9",
    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>Acute psychological and physiological stress recruits two distinct, coordinated neuroendocrine axes that differ fundamentally in their anatomical wiring, signaling velocity, and biochemical mediators: the fast sympathomedullary (SAM) axis and the slower hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. When environmental stressors are appraised as threatening, neural inputs converge on the paraventricular nucleus (PVN) of the hypothalamus and the rostral ventrolateral medulla. Along the SAM axis, preganglionic sympathetic fibers traverse the splanchnic nerves to synapse directly upon neuroendocrine chromaffin cells in the adrenal medulla, triggering immediate exocytosis of catecholamines (approximately 80% epinephrine and 20% norepinephrine) into systemic circulation within seconds. Circulating epinephrine binds &alpha;- and &beta;-adrenergic receptors, rapidly stimulating tachycardia, increased myocardial contractility, bronchodilation, and glycogenolysis to support immediate fight-or-flight motor responses.</p>\n<p>Concurrently, neurosecretory parvocellular neurons in the hypothalamic PVN initiate the HPA cascade by releasing corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) and arginine vasopressin into the hypophyseal portal system. CRH stimulates corticotrophs in the anterior pituitary to synthesize and cleave pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) into adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). ACTH is released into venous circulation and binds melanocortin type 2 receptors ($MC_2R$) in the zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex, stimulating de novo steroidogenesis and systemic secretion of cortisol. Circulating cortisol diffuses across plasma membranes to bind cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptors (GR), modulating gene transcription to sustain hepatic gluconeogenesis, inhibit peripheral glucose uptake via GLUT4 suppression, and inhibit pro-inflammatory transcription factor NF-&kappa;B. Peak systemic cortisol concentrations occur approximately 15 to 30 minutes following stressor onset.</p>\n<p>To characterize the divergence between SAM and HPA kinetics, clinical investigators subjected 48 healthy young adults to the Trier Social Stress Test (TSST), consisting of a 5-minute mock job interview followed by 5 minutes of rapid serial subtraction before an unexpressive evaluative committee. Two hours prior to testing, participants were randomized to ingest either oral dexamethasone (0.5 mg, a synthetic glucocorticoid that crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly but exerts potent negative feedback on pituitary corticotrophs) or an identical placebo. Cardiovascular parameters, plasma catecholamines, plasma ACTH, and salivary free cortisol were quantified longitudinally (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Cardiovascular and neuroendocrine kinetic profiles across time points during the Trier Social Stress Test</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Experimental Cohort</th><th>Measurement Time Point</th><th>Heart Rate (beats / min)</th><th>Plasma Epinephrine (pg/mL)</th><th>Plasma ACTH (pg/mL)</th><th>Salivary Cortisol (nmol/L)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>TSST + Placebo</td><td>Baseline (&minus;15 min)</td><td>68 &plusmn; 4</td><td>44 &plusmn; 6</td><td>14 &plusmn; 3</td><td>4.6 &plusmn; 0.6</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TSST + Placebo</td><td>Peak Stress (+2 min)</td><td>106 &plusmn; 7</td><td>186 &plusmn; 16</td><td>48 &plusmn; 6</td><td>6.4 &plusmn; 0.7</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TSST + Placebo</td><td>Post-Stress (+20 min)</td><td>74 &plusmn; 5</td><td>56 &plusmn; 7</td><td>26 &plusmn; 4</td><td>22.8 &plusmn; 2.5</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TSST + Placebo</td><td>Recovery (+60 min)</td><td>69 &plusmn; 4</td><td>46 &plusmn; 5</td><td>15 &plusmn; 3</td><td>8.2 &plusmn; 1.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TSST + Dexamethasone</td><td>Baseline (&minus;15 min)</td><td>67 &plusmn; 4</td><td>42 &plusmn; 5</td><td>&lt;5 &plusmn; 1</td><td>&lt;1.0 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TSST + Dexamethasone</td><td>Peak Stress (+2 min)</td><td>104 &plusmn; 6</td><td>182 &plusmn; 15</td><td>6 &plusmn; 2</td><td>1.2 &plusmn; 0.3</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TSST + Dexamethasone</td><td>Post-Stress (+20 min)</td><td>72 &plusmn; 4</td><td>52 &plusmn; 6</td><td>7 &plusmn; 2</td><td>1.4 &plusmn; 0.3</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TSST + Dexamethasone</td><td>Recovery (+60 min)</td><td>68 &plusmn; 4</td><td>43 &plusmn; 5</td><td>&lt;5 &plusmn; 1</td><td>&lt;1.0 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
