    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 6 (Questions 31&ndash;36)</div>\n<p>The Transactional Model of Coping formulated by Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman conceptualizes coping as an individual's dynamic cognitive and behavioral efforts to manage internal or external demands appraised as taxing or exceeding personal resources. The framework differentiates between two fundamental functional coping orientations:</p>\n<p>1. <em>Problem-Focused Coping</em>: Strategies directly aimed at managing, altering, or resolving the external source of stress. Examples include active problem-solving, structured time management, seeking informational guidance, and developing practical action plans. According to the \"goodness-of-fit\" hypothesis, problem-focused coping is most adaptive when stressors are appraised as objectively controllable.</p>\n<p>2. <em>Emotion-Focused Coping</em>: Strategies aimed at regulating, modulating, or alleviating the internal negative affective distress elicited by the stressor. Examples include cognitive reappraisal, active acceptance, seeking emotional social support, meditation, and positive reframing. Emotion-focused strategies are crucial when encountering uncontrollable stressors (such as terminal bereavement or unexpected corporate downsizing), where modifying the external event is impossible.</p>\n<p>Coping responses are further classified into adaptive versus maladaptive categories. Adaptive coping involves cognitive flexibility and productive emotional processing. In contrast, maladaptive coping includes behavioral disengagement, denial, substance abuse, and perseverative rumination. Rumination involves compulsively, repetitively focusing on the symptoms, causes, and distressing consequences of one's negative affect without engaging in active problem-solving. Neuroimaging reveals that rumination hyperactivates the default mode network (DMN), impairs executive working memory in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, and causes sustained, unremitting hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis hyperactivation.</p>\n<p>Investigators conducted an 8-week randomized controlled trial with 180 corporate employees facing corporate downsizing. Participants were randomly allocated to an Adaptive Reappraisal &amp; Problem-Solving Training Group, an Unstructured Emotional Venting Group, or a Waitlist Control Group. Assessments were conducted at Baseline (Week 0) and Post-Intervention (Week 8) to quantify Perceived Stress (PSS-10), Rumination Response Scale (RRS), and 24-hour urinary free cortisol (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Psychological distress, rumination scores, and 24-hour urinary free cortisol pre- and post-intervention</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Intervention Cohort</th><th>Assessment Time Point</th><th>Perceived Stress (PSS-10 Score)</th><th>Rumination (RRS Score)</th><th>24-Hour Urinary Cortisol (&mu;g / 24 h)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Adaptive Reappraisal Group</td><td>Baseline (Week 0)</td><td>28.6 &plusmn; 2.2</td><td>56.4 &plusmn; 4.2</td><td>58.6 &plusmn; 4.8</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Adaptive Reappraisal Group</td><td>Post-Intervention (Week 8)</td><td>14.2 &plusmn; 1.4</td><td>31.4 &plusmn; 2.5</td><td>32.4 &plusmn; 3.1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Unstructured Venting Group</td><td>Baseline (Week 0)</td><td>28.1 &plusmn; 2.1</td><td>55.9 &plusmn; 3.9</td><td>57.9 &plusmn; 5.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Unstructured Venting Group</td><td>Post-Intervention (Week 8)</td><td>30.2 &plusmn; 2.5</td><td>65.2 &plusmn; 4.8</td><td>66.8 &plusmn; 5.9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Waitlist Control Group</td><td>Baseline (Week 0)</td><td>28.3 &plusmn; 2.0</td><td>56.1 &plusmn; 4.1</td><td>58.2 &plusmn; 4.9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Waitlist Control Group</td><td>Post-Intervention (Week 8)</td><td>27.8 &plusmn; 2.2</td><td>55.4 &plusmn; 4.0</td><td>57.1 &plusmn; 5.1</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
