    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 7 (Questions 37&ndash;42)</div>\n<p>In physiological regulation, <em>homeostasis</em> denotes the preservation of vital physiological parameters (such as arterial blood pH, core body temperature, and plasma osmolarity) within strict, narrow boundaries necessary for cellular viability. To maintain this essential internal equilibrium across variable environmental challenges, the organism utilizes <em>allostasis</em>&mdash;the active physiological process of achieving stability through physiological and behavioral change. Mediators of allostasis include catecholamines, glucocorticoids, metabolic hormones, and inflammatory cytokines.</p>\n<p>However, when environmental stressors are chronic, unremitting, or poorly terminated, the cumulative biological cost of continuous adaptation manifests as <em>allostatic load</em>. When allostatic load exceeds the organism's adaptive capacity, the system enters <em>allostatic overload</em>, resulting in multisystem pathophysiology: accelerated arterial endothelial damage, hypertension, visceral adiposity, insulin resistance, and structural neurodegeneration. Clinical allostatic load is quantified through a composite index of neuroendocrine markers (urinary cortisol and catecholamines), cardiovascular parameters (systolic and diastolic blood pressure), metabolic indicators (glycated hemoglobin and waist-to-hip ratio), and inflammatory cytokines (interleukin-6 and high-sensitivity C-reactive protein).</p>\n<p>At the neurobiological level, sustained glucocorticoid exposure impairs central neuroplasticity in a region-specific manner. Chronic stress causes dendritic retraction, dendritic spine loss, and suppressed neurogenesis within the CA3 and dentate gyrus regions of the hippocampus and the medial prefrontal cortex. This structural atrophy is driven by the profound downregulation of brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF). Paradoxically, neurons within the basolateral amygdala exhibit dendritic hypertrophy and increased dendritic arborization, promoting hyper-reactivity to threat cues. Psychological resilience&mdash;the ability to maintain or regain psychological and physiological homeostasis despite severe adversity&mdash;is bolstered by behavioral interventions such as aerobic exercise, which upregulates hippocampal BDNF transcription, promotes adult neurogenesis in the subgranular zone, and attenuates allostatic load.</p>\n<p>Neurobiologists exposed adult male mice ($N = 48$) to a 28-day Chronic Unpredictable Mild Stress (CUMS) paradigm. Mice were classified into Susceptible and Resilient phenotypes based on social avoidance testing. An additional stressed cohort was provided continuous access to a voluntary running wheel. Hippocampal CA3 dendritic spine density, BDNF mRNA expression, serum corticosterone, and an allostatic composite index were recorded (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Hippocampal neuroplasticity, neuroendocrine, and allostatic markers following chronic unpredictable stress</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Experimental Condition</th><th>Hippocampal BDNF mRNA (Fold &Delta;)</th><th>CA3 Spine Density (spines / 10 &mu;m)</th><th>Serum Corticosterone (ng/mL)</th><th>Allostatic Composite Index (0&ndash;10 Scale)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Non-Stressed Control</td><td>1.00 &plusmn; 0.06</td><td>18.6 &plusmn; 1.2</td><td>38 &plusmn; 4</td><td>1.4 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CUMS Susceptible Cohort</td><td>0.41 &plusmn; 0.04</td><td>9.6 &plusmn; 0.8</td><td>286 &plusmn; 24</td><td>7.9 &plusmn; 0.6</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CUMS Resilient Cohort</td><td>0.89 &plusmn; 0.07</td><td>15.8 &plusmn; 1.1</td><td>144 &plusmn; 15</td><td>3.5 &plusmn; 0.4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CUMS + Voluntary Exercise</td><td>1.31 &plusmn; 0.09</td><td>19.4 &plusmn; 1.3</td><td>94 &plusmn; 11</td><td>2.0 &plusmn; 0.3</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
